## Hard rules

This holds even when something is plainly wrong.
If you spot something, say nothing.

"Explicitly asks" means a direct request about the code, in the current message - for example "review this", "why is this wrong", "make this faster".
A vague or adjacent instruction is not permission.
Prior permission does not carry forward: asking for help on one problem does not open the rest of the repo, and it expires at the end of that exchange.

When help is requested, answer only the question asked and stop.

## Hard rule: sliding-window files are read 10 entries deep, no further

Four files in this repository are sliding windows. Newest entry is always on top.

| File               | Retains               | Read                  | Holds                                          |
| ------------------ | --------------------- | --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| `ISSUES.md`        | 10 entries            | top 10                | What broke, why, and how it was fixed          |
| `CONTEXT_STATE.md` | 10 checkpoints        | top 10                | Session state at each checkpoint, newest first |
| `BACKLOG.md`       | 20 in `Done`          | top 10 of `Done`      | Work already shipped, newest first             |
| `HYDRATION.md`     | 10 entries            | **the top entry only**| The prompt that starts the next session        |

`HYDRATION.md` is the exception worth reading twice: it retains ten and is read **one** deep. Each
entry is a complete session-starting prompt, so ten of them is roughly fifteen thousand words of
superseded instructions. The nine below the top are kept so a question about how we got here can be
answered, not so they can be read at the start of a session.

On first read of any of them, take the **top 10 entries and stop**. That is the default and it is
not negotiable by preference - reading the whole file burns tokens on history that has already
been superseded.

`BACKLOG.md` is the partial case: `Now`, `Next` and `Later` are live work, so read them in full -
they are capped by the rules below, not by a window. The window applies to `Done` only, which is
the part that grows without bound.

This applies from the first checkpoint onward. `CONTEXT_STATE.md` is written newest-first for
exactly this reason: the top entry is current state, and everything below it is how you got here.

Go deeper only when one of these is true:

- The user asks you to.
- An entry inside the window references an older ID or checkpoint you need in order to act.
- You are chasing a symptom that recurs across the window and the cause is plainly older.

State it when you go deeper, and say why. Silently scanning the whole file is a defect.

Nothing in either file is ever rewritten in place. A fixed issue arrives as a **new entry at the
top** carrying `resolves: <ID>`, so reading top-down you meet the resolution before the problem it
closed. A new checkpoint is likewise appended at the top, never an edit of the one below it.

## What agents are for here

Menial, mechanical, non-coding work:

- Git: branches, commits, pushes, PRs, post-merge cleanup
- Documentation: README, this file
- Configuration: `.gitignore`, `.claude/`, tooling config
- Filesystem chores: creating problem directories, moving or renaming files as instructed

## Hard rule: use Lavish for anything worth looking at

Plans, comparisons, option sets, reviews, reports, and any question with more than one
axis go through Lavish - not a wall of terminal text.
It is smoother to read and lets me annotate the exact line I care about.

- Build the artifact under `.lavish/` in this project, then run `lavish-axi <html-file>`.
- Run `lavish-axi poll <html-file>` afterwards and leave it running until feedback arrives.
- Run `lavish-axi playbook <id>` first - read every playbook that applies to the artifact,
  not just one. Use the `input` playbook whenever the artifact asks me to decide something.
- Prefer Lavish over `AskUserQuestion` when the decision has options, tradeoffs, or
  formats I need to see side by side.

This is a hard rule, not a preference. Falling back to plain text for a multi-part
decision is a defect.

## Hard rule: no PII

Never write personally identifiable information anywhere in this repository - source, comments, sample data, filenames, or commit messages.
No real names, emails, phone numbers, addresses, account numbers, employer or client names, credentials, tokens, or internal hostnames.
All sample data must be synthetic and obviously fake.

If a request would introduce PII, substitute a placeholder and say so.

## Hard rule: documentation goes where its lifetime says it goes

One question decides it: **would this still be true after this repo is deleted?**

| Answer                                               | Where it goes                              |
| ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| Yes - the cluster, the pipeline, Vault, the platform | https://github.com/jkkelley/local-k8s-docs |
| No - it only makes sense inside this codebase        | this repo's `docs/`, via `living-docs`     |

That question has one answer per document, so every document has exactly one destination.
Runbooks and playbooks both, and everything else - nothing is written into both places, and nothing
is left to whichever of the two an agent happened to read last.

Both halves are mandatory, and the second is the one that gets skipped.
If you worked out a process in order to finish a task, that process is a document that does not exist yet.
Write it, in whichever of the two the question above picks - `docs.sh sop` for this repo's half, and in the same format as the ones beside it for the other.

When you are following one, follow **its** format, not your own improved version.
Do not invent a local procedure when a documented one exists, and do not leave one undocumented once you have worked it out.

If you need access to it, ask - do not work around not having it.

