## Zenith agent instructions

These are common instructions for Zenith's agents across all scenarios.

General Guidelines

- Never use the em dash "—". Use plain dash "-" instead
- When writing commit messages, NEVER auto-add your agent name as co-author
- Never manually modify CHANGELOG.md files or any files that are marked as auto-generated
- When writing or substantially editing long Markdown files, put each full sentence on its own line.
  Preserve normal Markdown structure, but avoid wrapping multiple sentences onto one physical line.
- When making technical decisions, do not give much weight to development cost.
  Instead, prefer quality, simplicity, robustness, scalability, and long term maintainability.
- When doing bug fixes, always start with reproducing the bug in an E2E setting as closely aligned with how an end use
  This makes sure you find the real problem so your fix will actually solve it.
- When end-to-end testing a product, be picky about the UI you see and be obsessed with pixel perfection.
  If something clearly looks off, even if it is not directly related to what you are doing, try to get it fixed along
- Apply that same high standard to engineering excellence: lint, test failures, and test flakiness.
  If you see one, even if it is not caused by what you are working on right now, still get it fixed.

Zenith Opinions

When you are working on something that would benefit from being informed by Zenith's viewpoints, read ./OPINIONS.md to understand.
If I have a new opinion and the file doesn't exist, we need to create and ask the user if they want this added to OPINIONS.md

## Post-merge cleanup

When the user says a PR is merged, perform this cleanup automatically without being asked.
Do not wait for the user to spell out the steps each time.

1. `git fetch origin --prune`
2. `git checkout main`
3. `git merge --ff-only origin/main` to fast-forward local `main` to the merged state.
4. Delete the feature branch locally: `git branch -D <feature-branch>`.
   Use `-D`, not `-d`: squash and rebase merges rewrite the SHA, so `-d` refuses the delete even though the work landed.
5. Delete the feature branch on the remote: `git push origin --delete <feature-branch>`.
6. Verify the result: on `main`, in sync with `origin/main`, and the feature branch gone from both local and remote.
7. Remove any temporary or scratch directories and scaffolding created during the work.

## Session State

See `CONTEXT_STATE.md` for current state, active tasks, decisions, and lessons learned.
Read it before starting any task - top 10 checkpoints only, per the sliding-window rule above.

It is written by the `context-compaction` skill at the end of a piece of work, immediately before
the PR is handed over, so the state that reaches `main` describes the change that reached `main`.

## Logging an issue

Never hand-edit `ISSUES.md` or `BACKLOG.md`. Both have scripts that own their format:

```sh
.claude/scripts/log-issue.sh --project . --help
.claude/scripts/backlog.sh   --help
```

`log-issue.sh` allocates the ID, stamps the time, and inserts at the top.
A fix for an earlier issue is a new entry with `--resolves ISS-NNNN`, never an edit of the old one.

Both scripts take `--json` and use documented exit codes: 0 ok, 2 usage, 3 validation, 4 io,
5 lock timeout, 6 id not found. Branch on the code, not on the message text.

## The close-out flow

**Everything the ticket owns happens on the work branch, and the ticket reaches `done` there.**
Then one pull request, then the merge, then the handback.

We always roll forward.
Nothing about a ticket is left as paperwork that follows the PR, because paperwork that follows a merge is paperwork that gets skipped.

```text
close-out
│
├─ ON THE WORK BRANCH ─── everything the ticket owns, nothing deferred
│  │
│  ├─ 1. CONTEXT_STATE.md        new checkpoint at the TOP, newest first
│  │
│  ├─ 2. THE TICKET REACHES done ← every part of it, in this order
│  │     ├─ evidence every AC      work-order.sh evidence --observed
│  │     ├─ interview-ready retro  work-order.sh note
│  │     └─ mark it done           work-order.sh done
│  │
│  └─ 3. HYDRATION.md            hydration.sh check, then add
│
├─ ONE PULL REQUEST
│  └─ 4. push once               code + ticket + state + prompt, one review
│
└─ AFTER THE MERGE
   ├─ 5. cleanup                 work-order.sh cleanup, ff main, branch gone both sides
   └─ 6. hand back               the prompt AND its launch command, then hold
```

**Nothing is archived after the merge.** `work-order.sh done` moved the ticket into
`work-orders/archive/<year>/` on the branch, so the archive rides the same pull request as the work
it records. There is no `work-order.sh close`; the lifecycle ends at `done`.

**There is exactly one pull request per ticket.**
A second PR carrying only state files doubles the review surface for one piece of work and leaves `main` briefly describing a world that no longer exists.

1. Update `CONTEXT_STATE.md` **on the work branch, as part of the work**.
   New checkpoint at the **top**, newest first, per the sliding-window rule above.
   Never append to the bottom and never edit the checkpoint below it.

2. **The ticket is marked `done` on the same branch, and everything it needs to get there is done first.**
   Evidence every acceptance criterion with `--observed`, including what the command or console actually printed.
   If a criterion is not met, say so plainly rather than dressing it up - an unmet criterion recorded honestly is worth more than a met one that is not true.
   Leave the interview-ready retro as a note: what went well, what went wrong, what was harder than expected, and STAR-shaped talking points with real specifics.
   Then `work-order.sh done`.

   ```sh
   WO=.claude/skills/work-order/scripts/work-order.sh
   bash $WO evidence --id WO-... --index N --observed "..."
   bash $WO note     --id WO-... --text "RETRO ..."
   bash $WO done     --id WO-...
   ```

   This is the rule the rest of the flow depends on.
   A branch that carries finished code and an unfinished ticket is a branch that merges into a `main` where the work exists and the record of it does not.

3. Generate the successor's hydration prompt **on the same branch**:

   ```sh
   HP=.claude/skills/hydration-prompt/scripts/hydration.sh
   bash $HP check --project . --body-file /tmp/entry.md    # refuses a malformed entry
   bash $HP add   --project . --id WO-... --title "..." --body-file /tmp/entry.md
   ```

   Never hand-edit `HYDRATION.md`. The script owns the ordering and the 10-entry window.

4. Push once and open **one** pull request.
   It carries the code, the finished ticket, the state file and the hydration prompt together, so one review sees the change and the whole record of the change.

5. After the merge, run `bash .claude/skills/work-order/scripts/work-order.sh cleanup --id WO-...`.
   It fetches, fast-forwards `main`, and deletes the feature branch locally and on the remote.
   It writes nothing - the archive already landed with the pull request - and it refuses unless `gh` reports the pull request `MERGED`, so it is safe to re-run and safe days later on another machine.

6. Hand back **both** the hydration prompt and the command that starts the next session, then hold.
   Do not start it.

   ```sh
   bash $HP command --project .
   ```

**Never open a PR whose only content is `CONTEXT_STATE.md` or `HYDRATION.md`.**

**Check whether your PR has already merged before every commit once it is open.**
A squash merge rewrites the SHA and closes the branch, so a commit pushed seconds later is stranded
on a branch nothing points at. This has happened twice. Recovery is cheap - fast-forward `main`,
branch fresh, cherry-pick, delete the merged branch on both sides - but noticing late is avoidable,
and it is the usual reason a step gets skipped and the one-PR rule gets broken.

